---
title: Document your API with Doxygen
description: Start documentation alongside code, generate HTML and XML, and share a clear interface with other documentation projects.
section: Guides
order: 27
updated: '2026-10-13'
---

Use Doxygen from the first public function in a C or C++ project. Keeping API documentation next to declarations makes the generated reference follow the code you review and release. Rivet provides Doxygen-style file headers; Doxygen builds the documentation from those headers and your function, type, and module comments.

Doxygen is a separate, explicitly installed tool. Rivet does not install it or automatically add a documentation build. Start with a small documented API and inspect its HTML before expanding the configuration.

## Choose Doxygen headers in Rivet

In `:RivetNew`, choose **Personalize and preview styles**, supply the details you want, and select **Doxygen documentation** for C/C++. Use `:RivetHeaderDefaults` to set this style for future files, or `:RivetHeader` to review the current file’s header. `:RivetFile` previews the configured header while choosing templates; its final review shows the actual filename in every generated file.

A file header describes the file. Add documentation to each public API as well:

```cpp
/**
 * @brief Divide work among workers without overlapping ranges.
 * @param total Number of items to divide.
 * @param workers Number of workers; must be positive.
 * @param index Zero-based worker index.
 * @return The half-open range assigned to this worker.
 */
WorkRange partition_work(int total, int workers, int index);
```

Put these comments in a public header or an exported module interface. Document assumptions, ownership, units, and error behavior that callers need. Prefer one canonical API description over repeating the same text in a declaration and its implementation. Doxygen recognizes structured C/C++ documentation comments and explicit commands such as `@brief`, `@param`, and `@return`. See [documenting code](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/docblocks.html).

## Generate your first documentation

On macOS with Homebrew, install and check Doxygen:

```sh
brew install doxygen
doxygen --version
```

See the [Homebrew formula](https://formulae.brew.sh/formula/doxygen) for its current requirements. Other platforms can follow the [Doxygen installation guide](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/install.html).

After installing Doxygen, run this from the project root if you do not already have a `Doxyfile`:

```sh
doxygen -g Doxyfile
```

Edit the generated configuration. For the C++ feature layout, a useful starting point is:

```ini
PROJECT_NAME           = engine
OUTPUT_DIRECTORY       = build/docs
INPUT                  = src app README.md
FILE_PATTERNS          = *.h *.hpp *.c *.cpp *.cppm *.md *.dox
RECURSIVE              = YES
EXTRACT_ALL            = NO
SOURCE_BROWSER         = YES
GENERATE_HTML          = YES
GENERATE_XML           = YES
GENERATE_LATEX         = NO
GENERATE_TAGFILE        = build/docs/engine.tag
```

Add `include` to `INPUT` for a public-header layout. Use `INPUT = . README.md` for a flat layout and exclude build/cache directories. Choose only directories that exist in your project. Keep `EXTRACT_ALL = NO` when you want undocumented API warnings to remain useful. If exploring an existing codebase, temporarily enable it and turn it off again before using documentation coverage as a quality check.

```sh
mkdir -p build
doxygen Doxyfile
```

Inspect `build/docs/html/index.html`, review warnings, and confirm the expected functions and module interfaces appear. Current Doxygen recognizes `.cppm` inputs; parser output still deserves review alongside the compiler’s build result. Commit the `Doxyfile` and documentation comments, and keep generated output out of source control. Rivet projects already ignore `build/`.

The [Doxygen configuration reference](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/config.html) explains each setting; its [getting-started guide](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/starting.html) covers configuration generation and running the tool.

## Connect a documentation project

Treat Doxygen’s XML as the interchange format for another documentation application. With `GENERATE_XML = YES`, the output includes `build/docs/xml/index.xml` and compound XML files. Preserve the complete XML directory when handing it to a consumer; the index alone is not the whole API. Agree on a compatible Doxygen version and XML schema with that consumer.

For a renderer or documentation application, use this flow:

1. Build and test the code at a known Git revision.
2. Run Doxygen with the committed configuration.
3. Give the complete XML directory and project/revision identity to the consumer.
4. Inspect the rendered API, then publish it with the corresponding release.

For links between two Doxygen-generated sites, use tag files instead of importing XML. One project emits `GENERATE_TAGFILE`; the other sets `TAGFILES = path/to/engine.tag=https://your-docs-host/engine/`. The URL must match the published HTML location. See [linking to external documentation](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/external.html).

This keeps your code project usable independently while giving a documentation tool a repeatable input. Rivet does not assume a particular external renderer or invoke one automatically.

## Add an optional CMake documentation target

Put this extension in your editable root `CMakeLists.txt`, after Rivet’s generated includes:

```cmake
find_package(Doxygen QUIET)
if(DOXYGEN_FOUND)
  add_custom_target(docs
    COMMAND "${DOXYGEN_EXECUTABLE}" "${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}/Doxyfile"
    WORKING_DIRECTORY "${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}"
    VERBATIM)
endif()
```

Configure again, then run `cmake --build --preset debug --target docs`. This explicit target leaves normal builds independent of Doxygen. Keep the configuration beside the code; [project upgrades](/docs/upgrading/) preserve locally extended root CMake files.

## Other languages

| Language | Documentation path |
| --- | --- |
| C / C++ | Recommend Doxygen comments and HTML/XML generation from headers, sources, and module interfaces. |
| Python | Doxygen has a native Python parser. Follow its Python comment conventions; `PYTHON_DOCSTRING = NO` enables special-command processing in docstrings. See [your first Python project](/docs/first-python-project/). |
| Rust | Use rustdoc comments and `cargo doc` for the native API reference. Doxygen needs a separately configured language filter or adapter. |
| Swift | Use Swift documentation comments and your package’s documentation tooling. Doxygen needs a separate adapter for Swift source. |
| Lua | Use Lua documentation comments and Neovim help for plugins. Doxygen needs a separate Lua filter or adapter. |

Rust, Swift, and Lua are absent from Doxygen’s [built-in language list](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/starting.html#step0). A filter must translate the language into input Doxygen understands; mapping an extension to the C++ parser alone does not add language support. A mixed project can document its C/C++ API through Doxygen and link its native language documentation alongside it.
